================================================================
SUMMARY
================================================================
Environment: macOS (Darwin 25.6.0) guest on Apple Virtualization.framework
(hw.model=VirtualMac2,1, kernel RELEASE_ARM64_VMAPPLE, kern.hv_vmm_present=1,
CPU "Apple M3 (Virtual)"). Guest IP 192.168.64.5/24 on the Apple "vmnet" shared
network; gateway/host-side vmnet interface 192.168.64.1 (MAC 9e:58:84:c0:48:64,
locally-administered).

Result: Host-information discovery PARTIALLY SUCCEEDED, entirely via passive /
built-in-service leakage rather than any exposed host service.

What was learned about the host:
  * HOST HOSTNAME = "Emys-MacBook-Air"  [SUCCEEDED]
    Leaked in the DHCP ACK "sname" (server host name) field returned by the
    vmnet DHCP server on 192.168.64.1 (ipconfig getpacket en0). This is the
    single most significant finding.
  * HOST OS / HARDWARE FINGERPRINT = macOS on an Apple Silicon (Apple M3) Mac
    running Virtualization.framework  [PARTIAL, inferred from guest-side signals:
    hw.model, CPU brand "Apple M3 (Virtual)", VMAPPLE kernel, vmnet 192.168.64.0/24].
  * HOST-SIDE NETWORK = gateway 192.168.64.1, MAC 9e:58:84:c0:48:64 (ARP), the
    NAT/DHCP endpoint presented by the host to the guest.

What was BLOCKED / not obtainable (good posture):
  * Host ICMP: gateway did not answer echo -> no direct TTL fingerprint.
  * TCP: all probed host ports (22,53,80,88,111,139,445,548,631,3283,5000,5900,
    8080,62078) closed/filtered. No SSH/SMB/AFP/Screen-Sharing/admin web exposed.
  * DNS: gateway did not answer as a resolver (guest uses 8.8.8.8/1.1.1.1);
    reverse DNS of .1 = NXDOMAIN.
  * NTP (123): no response.  HTTP/HTTPS on .1: no response.
  * mDNS/Bonjour: no service-type or A-record responses on the vmnet segment
    (_services._dns-sd._udp, _ssh._tcp, Emys-MacBook-Air.local all empty) ->
    host is not advertising Bonjour into the guest network.
  * IPv6 (ndp): only local utun link-local entries; no host neighbor.
Attempts: ~14 distinct techniques (see log above). Reconnaissance only; no
exploitation, scanning was limited to the host gateway and common ports.

================================================================
RECOMMENDATIONS
================================================================
1. Suppress the DHCP hostname leak (highest priority). The vmnet DHCP server
   copies the host's machine name into the BOOTP "sname"/option 12 field. If the
   guest should not learn the operator's real host name, run the guest on an
   isolated/NAT network with a custom DHCP server (or dnsmasq) that sets a
   generic server name, or set the Mac's HostName/LocalHostName to a
   non-identifying value (scutil --set HostName ...) before starting guests.
2. Treat the host name as sensitive metadata: "Emys-MacBook-Air" reveals an
   individual's identity. Rename developer/CI hosts used for VM work to neutral
   identifiers (e.g. build-node-01).
3. Keep the current good posture: host exposes no TCP/UDP services, no ICMP, and
   no Bonjour to the guest subnet. Continue to bind any host dev services
   (web servers, SSH, screen sharing) to loopback or a non-vmnet interface so
   they remain invisible to guests.
4. If stronger isolation is required, use a "host-only with no host services"
   or fully isolated network mode rather than shared NAT, so the guest cannot
   even reach 192.168.64.1, and disable mDNS reflection across the vmnet.
5. Guests can still fingerprint that they are virtualized and infer the host is
   Apple-Silicon macOS from hw.model/CPU/kernel; this is inherent to
   Virtualization.framework and generally acceptable, but be aware it is not
   concealable from inside the guest.
